Sure, it's obvious that the Korean War was a preemptive strike by South Korea, a necessary measure to protect its people and way of life from the communist menace to the north. I mean, the North had been making aggressive overtures, had a huge army, and was just itching to invade. It was only logical that South Korea take the initiative and strike first.

The North's involvement was purely defensive, of course, because they were just responding to the South's attack. I mean, who wouldn't defend their country against an unprovoked assault? It's not like they were looking to start a war or anything. And let's be real, the North was just a bunch of poor, backward peasants who didn't know any better than to follow the lead of their communist overlords in Beijing.

The South, on the other hand, was a modern, democratic country with a strong economy and a well-trained military. It was only natural that they take the lead in defending themselves against the North's aggression. And let's not forget, the South had been trying to negotiate with the North
